\title{Adaptive Soundness}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-19}

\p{
    Whether the statement is fixed before the hash function is public or chosen after it is the qualifier the [Fiat–Shamir](dhsorens-002G) attacks turn on, and it is usually left implicit. This page should make the distinction explicit and say which side deployed claims are on.
}

\p{
    Questions this page should answer:
    \ul{
        \li{Non-adaptive and adaptive soundness stated side by side, for both the plain and the [knowledge](dhsorens-004J) versions.}
        \li{Why the [Khovratovich–Rothblum–Soukhanov attack](dhsorens-004X) is an adaptive-soundness
        attack: it embeds the hash function into the circuit, which is a statement chosen after the
        hash is known.}
        \li{What adaptivity costs in the random oracle model, and whether the loss is a constant, a factor of the query count, or worse.}
        \li{Block, Garreta, Tiwari, and Zając: a special-sound IOP Fiat–Shamir-compiles to an adaptively sound non-interactive proof in the QROM. What that covers and what it does not.}
        \li{Whether a [zkVM](dhsorens-003Q)'s statement is genuinely fixed at circuit-compile time in deployment — including [precompiles](dhsorens-003V) and any circuit chosen by an untrusted party — and who is responsible for checking.}
    }
}

\p{
    Sources: [How to Prove False Statements](https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/118), [On Soundness Notions for Interactive Oracle Proofs](https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/1256).
}
